  "source_dataset": "MIMIC_IV_NOTE",
  "text": "Allergies:\nLatex / lisinopril / finasteride\n\nChief Complaint:\nconfusion\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ yo man with a histroy of nonischemic cardiomyopathy with an\nejection fraction of approximately 20% to 30%, Paroxysmal atrial\nfibrillation on coumadin, ventricular tachycardia and \nventricular\nfibrillation, ___ dual-chamber ICD s/p syncopal episode, VT\nablation ___, admission in ___ for new diagnosis\nundifferentiated cirrhosis p/w confusion. Patient was in normal\nstate of health last night. \n\nStarted shaking this morning. He felt very fatigued at that \ntime.\nHe also had an episode of dry heaving and bloating. Denies:\nfevers, headache, runny nose, sore throat, neck pain, chest \npain,\nsob, coughing, abdominal pain, urinary symptoms, increased or\ndecreased bowel movements, rash. \n\nHe states that he has been taking his medications daily. He\nstates that he has diarrhea all day, comes out like water. He\nsays he has ___ bowel movements a day, watery. He denies any\nblood coming from above or below. All of this history was\nconfirmed by his wife.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\nBRIEF SUMMARY:\n==============\nMr. ___ is a ___ yo man with complicated cardiac history \nnotable for HFrEF EF 20% to 30% previously and now improved to \n50%, paroxysmal atrial fibrillation on Coumadin, and ventricular \ntachycardia(s/p ICD), with newly diagnosed cirrhosis in ___, \nwho comes in for confusion, consistent with HE. His \nhospitalization remained without a clear precipitant, but \nhypotension/dehydration was suspected as a cause.\n\nACUTE/ACTIVE ISSUES:\n====================\n# HE\n# AMS: \nPatient was noted to have asterixis on admission, along with \nconfusion. Patient states that he has frequent bowel movement \n___ times a day while wife says only 5 times a day. He does \nhave ___ and hyponatremia that can trigger HE. Lower concern \nfor infection given lack of localizing symptoms and negative \ninfectious work-up. He had only trace ascites and no tappable \npocket. Lower concern for biliary infection. U/S was concerning \nfor a PVT on this admission but on last admission, initial u/s \nalso revealed high concern for PVT but with subsequent CT \nexonerating clot. He was given lactulose q2h for several days \nwith improvement in mental status. Initially kept on lactulose \n30mg TID and rifaximin after this, but continued to have 5 BMs a \nday, so decreased to 15mL TID on discharge. He remained without \nfurther signs of HE and had no flap on discharge.\n\n# Cirrhosis: \nRecent diagnosis of cirrhosis in ___ ___/p and \nultrasound concerning for cirrhotic liver, ascites, \nsplenomegaly. Most likely etiology thought during ___ \nhospital admission was cardiac in nature given history of HFrEF. \nDoes have an alcohol use history. Hbs Ab positive, smooth muscle \npositive, ___ negative, HCV negative.\n-Volume: \nHe was given albumin throughout his stay due to suspected \nhypovolemia i/s/o frequent BMs and ___. Diuretics were held for \nthis reason as well. At discharge, patient was euvolemic, with \nno pitting edema or JVP, so his diuretics were held.\n-Infection: \nNo prior history of SBP. Trace ascites without tappable pocket. \nDid not receive antibiotics.\n-Bleeding: \nNo varices seen on EGD ___, no PPI/CTX/octreotide during \ncourse\n-Encephalopathy: \nAs above with lactulose and rifaxamin \n-Screening: \nNo lesions meeting OPTN 5 criteria for hepatocellular carcinoma \non CT a/p ___\n-Transplant: \nhas not been evaluated \n\n# Leukocytosis: \nOn admission 14.2, neutrophil 90.2% without bands. Infectious \nwas negative. No localizing symptoms. Ultimately his WBC \ndowntrended and he remained without signs of infection.\n\n# Concern for PVT: \nRUQUS on ___ without visualization of flow within the main \nportal vein with reversal of flow within the L portal vein and \nsplenic vein concerning for PVT. This was similar to U/S \nfindings on last admission ___xonerated \nPVT. Most likely, slow flow in portal system leading to this \nfinding suggestive of PVT on U/S. Initially started on heparin \nfor coverage in case of clot through Coumadin, but d/c'd. \nBecause the patient clinically improved with medication \ntitration, no further imaging was done and he was continued on \nhome dose of warfarin.\n\n# ___ on CKD stage III (Solitary functioning kidney: \nHas known stage III CKD with Cr around 1.1. On admission, Cr \n1.6. Was able to void on his own without retention. He was given \nalbumin throughout the hospital course to replete losses through \nfrequent bowel movements. His creatinine fluctuated around \n1.4-1.6 during his hospital course with improvement in his BUN. \nUltimately, he maintained good urine outpatient and his \ncreatinine was suspected to be elevated to a new baseline. His \ndiurectics and ACE were held at discharge.\n\n# Normocytic anemia: \nnormocytic anemia since ___ without evidence of iron \ndeficiency. Hgb on admission 10.4, which is slightly lower than \nbaseline of ___. Patient denies black or bloody stools which \nthe wife confirms. No varices on recent EGD ___. Low concern \nfor GI bleed. \n\n# HFpEF: \nPatient had a known history of non ischemic cardiomyopathy. TTE \nin ___ showed EF ___ with severe TR but a repeat TTE \ndemonstrated LVEF of 50% on ___ TTE. \n-preload: Held furosemide and spironolactone iso ___\n-afterload: Held losartan\n-NHBK: Continued home metoprolol as below\n-preventative: Continued rosuvastatin\n-inotropy: none\n\n# Ventricular Tachycardia s/p ICD placement: \nPatient most recently had an episode of V tach in ___ VT @ \n151 bpm, ATP x 3 failed, 20j shock. Had an ablation done prior \nto that but none after the ___ episode. Patient has been \ntransitioned off of amiodarone recently given newly diagnosed \ncirrhosis. He was continued on metoprolol fractionated while in \nhouse. He did not have SVT while in house.\n\n# Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation (Chads VASC 2): \nPatient resented with a known history of paroxysmal atrial \nfibrillation. He had been maintained on Coumadin with a goal INR \nof ___. Initially on heparin, then maintained on home dose of \nwarfarin.\n\n# Hypertension: \nWhile patient was in house, his home losartan, furosemide, \nspironolactone given acute decompensation and concern for \nprerenal ___. His BPs remained in the low 100s despite holding \nhis home antihypertensives. Because of this and his euvolemic \nstatus, his spironolactone, furosemide, and losartan.\n\n # CODE: Presumed FULL \n # CONTACT: ___ (wife) - ___ ___ speaker),\n___ (dtr) - ___\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nPRIMARY\n-----------\nHepatic encephalopathy\nAcute kidney injury\n\nSECONDARY\n------------\nCirrhosis",
